WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.617
Unsafe code should come with a safety
contract.

2
00:00:02.697 --> 00:00:05.980
The contract explains what must be true
before the unsafe operation runs,

3
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.236
what invariants the code maintains, what
callers are responsible for,

4
00:00:09.316 --> 00:00:11.275
and what future maintainers must not
break.

5
00:00:11.355 --> 00:00:13.048
This is not paperwork for its own sake.

6
00:00:13.128 --> 00:00:15.511
It is the evidence that makes unsafe code
reviewable.

7
00:00:15.591 --> 00:00:18.767
A useful safety comment is not a
decorative note that says, this is safe.

8
00:00:18.847 --> 00:00:22.129
It explains why the unsafe operation is
valid under specific conditions.

9
00:00:22.209 --> 00:00:23.850
It may identify the owner of a buffer,

10
00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.636
the lifetime relationship being preserved,
the source of an alignment guarantee,

11
00:00:27.716 --> 00:00:31.527
the reason a pointer is non-null, or the
synchronization that protects shared state.

12
00:00:31.607 --> 00:00:34.942
Documentation should identify trusted
inputs, ownership expectations,

13
00:00:35.022 --> 00:00:38.887
lifetime assumptions, thread assumptions,
layout assumptions, and error behavior.

14
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:42.672
If a caller must pass a value created only
through a particular constructor, say so.

15
00:00:42.752 --> 00:00:45.611
If an external function must not retain a
pointer after the call,

16
00:00:45.691 --> 00:00:46.961
document that expectation.

17
00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:50.747
If a type representation must match an
external ABI, make that assumption visible.

18
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:53.050
Safety documentation also needs to stay
current.

19
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.730
Unsafe code can become risky when
surrounding code changes and the old comment no

20
00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.557
longer describes the real conditions.

21
00:00:58.637 --> 00:01:01.496
Reviewers should treat stale safety
comments as a warning sign,

22
00:01:01.576 --> 00:01:04.646
because outdated documentation can be
worse than no documentation:

23
00:01:04.726 --> 00:01:07.161
it creates confidence where the evidence
has drifted.

24
00:01:07.241 --> 00:01:10.205
Without safety contracts, unsafe code
becomes tribal knowledge,

25
00:01:10.285 --> 00:01:11.768
and tribal knowledge ages badly.

26
00:01:11.848 --> 00:01:15.553
People leave, APIs change, dependencies
update, and platform assumptions shift.

27
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.392
Documentation turns hidden reasoning into
shared engineering evidence that can be

28
00:01:19.472 --> 00:01:21.060
reviewed, tested, and maintained.
